WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:08.160
I am on a device, and I decided to look up my IP&nbsp;
address. This IP address is a class A address.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.680
How do I know this? Well, IP addresses and their&nbsp;
subnet masks fall into one of two types: classful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.560
or classless. Classful addresses have a pretty&nbsp;
organized structure for subnet masks, in that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:25.680
subnet mask number for each octet is either 0 or&nbsp;
255. Remember, the subnet mask dictates which part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.680
of an IP address is the network portion and which&nbsp;
part is the host portion. The first three classes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:37.600
A, B, and C, all represent IP addresses in&nbsp;
wide use on networks. Here are the particulars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.880
There are two more classes you need to know&nbsp;
about for the exam, classes D and E. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.680
are not used in the same vein as classes&nbsp;
A through C. We don't assign IP addresses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:53.520
using class D or class E addresses. Class D&nbsp;
addresses are used for multicast transmissions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:59.920
Class E addresses are used for research. Here are&nbsp;
the ranges of class D and class E IP addresses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.560
Going back and looking at these ranges, you may be&nbsp;
thinking, what about the first number of 127? An&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:14.480
address that uses 127 is a loopback or diagnostic&nbsp;
address and does not fit into this IP addressing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:19.920
scheme. Let's take a look at a classless IP&nbsp;
address. This IP address has a subnet mask of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:27.840
255.255.240.0, which means it does not fit into&nbsp;
the scheme of classful addresses we have covered.

